nodecode-game-editor is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-game-editor:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-game-editor
  commit 3a2b23e8a60714b4346b3d8f7c9c2a12b29718a8
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

The original is a Hermes plugin: a Python bridge that publishes eight MCP tools,
four of which read a Godot 4 project's files on disk (scene_look, script_look,
file_look, project_look) and four of which drive a running Godot editor through
the Godot AI add-on (editor_look, asset_look, plan_change, apply_plan, with
playing, testing and exporting the project). This rewrite keeps the four disk
reads, under the same tool names and the same ops, reimplemented in Common Lisp
against this harness's cell contract: the .tscn reader (scene tree, node search,
children, stored values, groups, connections), the .gd/.cs reader (source and
outline), the project walker (file text, file listing, resources) and the
project.godot reader (settings, autoloads, input map), plus a /game-editor
command and a manual topic. The path rules are the original's: res:// only,
nothing outside the project, nothing in a hidden file or folder, user://
refused.

What was left out, and why: the four tools that need a running Godot editor and
the Godot AI add-on -- plan_change and the two-approval apply_plan with their
prefab, overwrite and unsaved-work checks, place_assets for GameForge assets,
play_session, GUT/gdUnit4 checks and ship_build exports. They need a Godot
binary, a Python bridge on 127.0.0.1 and the add-on's own ports; a cell that
reads a project folder should not also be a client of an editor. The original's
planning, approval and effect tables are therefore not carried over, and this
cell writes nothing.
